const moduleBodies = {"abap": "<div class=\"detail-hero\"><img src=\"diagrams/abap-architecture.svg\" alt=\"AS ABAP Fundamentals &amp; System Start/Stop diagram\"></div>\n    <h2>AS ABAP Fundamentals &amp; System Start/Stop</h2>\n    <p class=\"lead\">Architecture, instances, work processes, start/stop procedures and log analysis.</p>\n    <h3>Step-by-step procedure</h3><div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">1</div><div><h4>Understand the SAP system layers</h4><p>Start with the three logical layers: presentation/client, application server and database. On the application layer, the dispatcher distributes requests to dialog/background/update/spool work processes. Central services such as message and enqueue services support communication and locking.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">2</div><div><h4>Identify the instance layout</h4><p>Document the SID, instance numbers, hostnames and instance roles. Typical landscapes have ASCS, one or more application server instances and the database. Do not assume every system has the same topology.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">3</div><div><h4>Check the operating system</h4><p>Before SAP troubleshooting, confirm the host is reachable, filesystem mounts are present, CPU/memory are reasonable and the SAP/HANA users and directories are available. A simple OS problem can look like an SAP problem.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">4</div><div><h4>Check database availability</h4><p>Confirm the database is running before repeatedly starting SAP. For HANA, use the approved HANA administration tools/commands for the installed release. If the DB is unavailable, the SAP dispatcher may not become healthy.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">5</div><div><h4>Start the SAP system</h4><p>Use the organisation's supported start method, such as SAPControl/service management. Start central services and database dependencies in the required sequence, then application instances. Monitor process status rather than assuming the start succeeded.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">6</div><div><h4>Validate processes</h4><p>Use <code>sapcontrol -nr NN -function GetProcessList</code> or the approved equivalent. Confirm required processes are GREEN/RUNNING and investigate any process in GRAY/YELLOW/RED.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">7</div><div><h4>Check startup logs</h4><p>Review dispatcher, message server, gateway and other relevant developer traces. Always correlate log timestamps with the start attempt. Look for permission, port, profile, filesystem and dependency errors.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">8</div><div><h4>Stop the SAP system</h4><p>Use a controlled stop procedure. Communicate impact, stop application workloads safely and follow the environment's dependency order. Avoid OS-level kill commands unless the approved recovery procedure requires them.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">9</div><div><h4>Validate shutdown</h4><p>Confirm SAP processes have stopped, ports are released and no orphan process remains. Record the shutdown time for audit and troubleshooting.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">10</div><div><h4>Build the incident timeline</h4><p>For any failed start/stop, capture: request time, command used, process state, first error timestamp, corrective action and final validation. This becomes the foundation of an RCA.</p></div></div></div>\n    <h3>Key T-codes / tools</h3><div class=\"codechips\"><code>SM51</code><code>SM50</code><code>SM66</code><code>SM21</code><code>ST22</code><code>ST11</code></div>\n    <h3>Topics covered from your syllabus</h3><ul class=\"source-list\"><li>AS ABAP Fundamentals</li><li>Starting and Stopping SAP Systems</li><li>Exercise 1: Start and Stop the SAP System</li><li>Exercise 2: Logs of the Start and Stop Processes of SAP AS ABAP System</li></ul>\n    <h3>Interview answer</h3><div class=\"callout\">I explain SAP start/stop as a controlled dependency exercise: verify OS and database first, start central services and application instances through the supported method, monitor process states, review traces if something fails, and validate user access and business transactions before declaring the system available.</div>\n    <h3>Production checklist</h3><ul><li>Capture exact timestamp and affected scope.</li><li>Check evidence before making a change.</li><li>Use approved change/incident procedures.</li><li>Validate the original business symptom after the fix.</li><li>Document RCA and preventive action for recurring issues.</li></ul>", "profiles": "<div class=\"detail-hero\"><img src=\"diagrams/profiles-operation-modes.svg\" alt=\"Technical System Configuration: Profiles, Parameters, Operation Modes &amp; Task Lists diagram\"></div>\n    <h2>Technical System Configuration: Profiles, Parameters, Operation Modes &amp; Task Lists</h2>\n    <p class=\"lead\">Profile management, RZ10/RZ11, operation modes and ABAP task lists.</p>\n    <h3>Step-by-step procedure</h3><div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">1</div><div><h4>Understand profile types</h4><p>Separate default, instance and start profiles conceptually. Know which parameters are global and which are instance-specific. Never change a parameter simply because a search result suggests it.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">2</div><div><h4>Read a parameter first</h4><p>Use RZ11 to inspect the current value, dynamic/static nature and documentation where available. Capture the current setting before making a change.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">3</div><div><h4>Plan a permanent change</h4><p>For a permanent profile change, use the approved profile-management process such as RZ10 or the operating procedure used by your SAP release. Record old value, new value, reason, change ticket and restart requirement.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">4</div><div><h4>Determine whether restart is required</h4><p>Some parameters can be changed dynamically; others require an instance/system restart. Treat the parameter's documented behavior as authoritative.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">5</div><div><h4>Validate after a change</h4><p>Recheck the parameter, verify the intended process behavior and inspect SM21/traces for side effects. Do not close the change based only on 'parameter saved'.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">6</div><div><h4>Understand operation modes</h4><p>Operation modes allow controlled allocation of work-process resources between different time periods or workloads. The goal is to match capacity to business demand.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">7</div><div><h4>Design day/night schedules</h4><p>Identify peak business hours, batch windows and maintenance periods. Allocate dialog/background resources accordingly and document the schedule.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">8</div><div><h4>Test operation-mode switching</h4><p>Validate the mode schedule in a non-production environment first. Observe work-process counts and background workload after the switch.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">9</div><div><h4>Use ABAP task lists</h4><p>Task lists package guided administration activities. Identify the task list, review the steps and parameters, execute only approved activities and review the execution log.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">10</div><div><h4>Treat configuration as change management</h4><p>Every production parameter, operation-mode or task-list execution should have an owner, purpose, validation criteria and rollback/mitigation plan.</p></div></div></div>\n    <h3>Key T-codes / tools</h3><div class=\"codechips\"><code>RZ10</code><code>RZ11</code><code>SM50</code><code>SM66</code><code>ST22</code><code>SM21</code></div>\n    <h3>Topics covered from your syllabus</h3><ul class=\"source-list\"><li>Technical System Configuration (Profile Management)</li><li>Setting profile parameters</li><li>Setting up and working with operation modes</li><li>Working with ABAP Task lists</li><li>Exercise 3: Determine the Values of Selected Profile Parameters</li><li>Exercise 4: Maintain System Parameters</li><li>Exercise 5: Setting Up Operation Modes</li><li>Exercise 6: Create and Execute a Task List</li></ul>\n    <h3>Interview answer</h3><div class=\"callout\">A good answer is: I first inspect the parameter and its documentation, determine whether it is dynamic or restart-dependent, record the current value, make the controlled change, validate the new behavior and monitor for side effects.</div>\n    <h3>Production checklist</h3><ul><li>Capture exact timestamp and affected scope.</li><li>Check evidence before making a change.</li><li>Use approved change/incident procedures.</li><li>Validate the original business symptom after the fix.</li><li>Document RCA and preventive action for recurring issues.</li></ul>", "database": "<div class=\"detail-hero\"><img src=\"diagrams/database-architecture.svg\" alt=\"Database Architecture, Services &amp; Administration diagram\"></div>\n    <h2>Database Architecture, Services &amp; Administration</h2>\n    <p class=\"lead\">Database architecture from a BASIS perspective and how SAP depends on the database.</p>\n    <h3>Step-by-step procedure</h3><div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">1</div><div><h4>Map SAP to the database</h4><p>SAP application processes send SQL/database requests to the database layer. BASIS must understand the boundary between application, database and operating system responsibilities.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">2</div><div><h4>Identify database type and version</h4><p>Record the database platform, version/revision, host, ports and supported SAP release combination. Compatibility is a prerequisite for maintenance and upgrades.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">3</div><div><h4>Check database availability</h4><p>Use the database's supported administration interface and SAP monitoring to confirm the database is online and accepting connections.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">4</div><div><h4>Check space</h4><p>Monitor database data volume, log volume, filesystem capacity and growth trend. Capacity issues are often progressive, so trend analysis matters.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">5</div><div><h4>Check backup status</h4><p>Confirm that full/data backups, log backups and retention are functioning according to the organisation's recovery design.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">6</div><div><h4>Correlate database and SAP symptoms</h4><p>A slow SAP transaction does not automatically mean a slow database. Use ST03N/STAD and database monitoring to determine where the response time is spent.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">7</div><div><h4>Separate logical and physical issues</h4><p>A SQL problem, table growth issue, filesystem problem and OS memory problem require different owners and fixes.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">8</div><div><h4>Document recovery dependencies</h4><p>Know what SAP requires from the database after a restart or recovery and which validation checks prove the system is ready.</p></div></div></div>\n    <h3>Key T-codes / tools</h3><div class=\"codechips\"><code>DB02</code><code>DB12</code><code>DB13</code><code>DBACOCKPIT</code><code>ST04</code><code>ST03N</code></div>\n    <h3>Topics covered from your syllabus</h3><ul class=\"source-list\"><li>Architecture of Database systems</li><li>Technical Architecture, Services &amp; Administration of SAP Databases</li></ul>\n    <h3>Interview answer</h3><div class=\"callout\">I treat the database as one layer in the end-to-end path. I first establish evidence that database time or health is actually the bottleneck, then work with the DBA team using exact timestamps and monitoring data.</div>\n    <h3>Production checklist</h3><ul><li>Capture exact timestamp and affected scope.</li><li>Check evidence before making a change.</li><li>Use approved change/incident procedures.</li><li>Validate the original business symptom after the fix.</li><li>Document RCA and preventive action for recurring issues.</li></ul>", "users-rfc": "<div class=\"detail-hero\"><img src=\"diagrams/user-rfc.svg\" alt=\"User Administration, Authorization &amp; RFC Communication diagram\"></div>\n    <h2>User Administration, Authorization &amp; RFC Communication</h2>\n    <p class=\"lead\">Users, authorization concepts, RFC destinations and secure system-to-system communication.</p>\n    <h3>Step-by-step procedure</h3><div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">1</div><div><h4>Classify the user issue</h4><p>Determine whether one user, one group, one client or all users are affected. This immediately narrows the investigation.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">2</div><div><h4>Check the user in SU01</h4><p>Review lock status, validity dates, user type, password status and assigned roles according to policy. Avoid changing access just to make an error disappear.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">3</div><div><h4>Understand authorization flow</h4><p>Think in terms of user → roles → authorization objects → field values → authorization check. A missing role and an incorrect organizational value can look similar to the user.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">4</div><div><h4>Use SUIM for analysis</h4><p>For audit/reporting-style questions, use SUIM to analyse users, roles and authorization assignments where supported.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">5</div><div><h4>Understand RFC components</h4><p>An RFC destination contains connection details and authentication information. The end-to-end path also depends on DNS/network/firewall, gateway and target-system availability.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">6</div><div><h4>Test SM59</h4><p>Run connection and authorization tests as appropriate. Capture the exact error and timestamp.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">7</div><div><h4>Check target side</h4><p>If SM59 fails, determine whether the target application instance, gateway, user, client or network path is the problem.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">8</div><div><h4>Handle CUA carefully</h4><p>For Central User Administration, understand the central system/distribution relationship and validate synchronization before assuming a user change has reached all child systems.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">9</div><div><h4>Secure credentials</h4><p>Use approved technical users and secure credential handling. Never place passwords in screenshots, scripts or tickets.</p></div></div></div>\n    <h3>Key T-codes / tools</h3><div class=\"codechips\"><code>SU01</code><code>SUIM</code><code>SM59</code><code>SMGW</code><code>SM04</code><code>AL08</code></div>\n    <h3>Topics covered from your syllabus</h3><ul class=\"source-list\"><li>Fundamentals of User Administration</li><li>Understanding the User administration concept</li><li>Understanding the AS ABAP Authorization concept</li><li>RFC Communication</li><li>Setting up RFC connections</li><li>Central User Administration (CUA)</li></ul>\n    <h3>Interview answer</h3><div class=\"callout\">For an RFC issue I do not stop at SM59. I test the destination, verify the technical user and target client, check target availability and gateway/network evidence, then retest the actual business interface.</div>\n    <h3>Production checklist</h3><ul><li>Capture exact timestamp and affected scope.</li><li>Check evidence before making a change.</li><li>Use approved change/incident procedures.</li><li>Validate the original business symptom after the fix.</li><li>Document RCA and preventive action for recurring issues.</li></ul>", "maintenance": "<div class=\"detail-hero\"><img src=\"diagrams/software-maintenance.svg\" alt=\"Software Maintenance: SAP Notes, SPAM/SAINT &amp; SUM diagram\"></div>\n    <h2>Software Maintenance: SAP Notes, SPAM/SAINT &amp; SUM</h2>\n    <p class=\"lead\">Plan, prepare, execute and validate SAP software maintenance.</p>\n    <h3>Step-by-step procedure</h3><div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">1</div><div><h4>Define the maintenance objective</h4><p>Clarify whether the activity is a SAP Note, Support Package, enhancement/package update or system upgrade. The tool and risk profile differ.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">2</div><div><h4>Check compatibility</h4><p>Confirm SAP product/release, kernel, database, add-ons and relevant support requirements before downloading or importing anything.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">3</div><div><h4>Prepare the change</h4><p>Create a plan covering backups, filesystem space, media, permissions, downtime, communication, validation and contingency.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">4</div><div><h4>SAP Note process</h4><p>Review the Note, prerequisites and implementation instructions. Use the appropriate SAP Note tooling for the release and confirm the correction is applicable.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">5</div><div><h4>SPAM/SAINT concept</h4><p>Understand Support Package Manager for support-package related processing and SAINT for add-on installation/update activities where applicable.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">6</div><div><h4>SUM preparation</h4><p>SUM requires preparation such as maintenance planning, stack information, prerequisites, cleanup and sufficient filesystem capacity. Exact phases vary by product/release.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">7</div><div><h4>Monitor SUM</h4><p>Follow SUM phase progress and logs. Do not restart or skip phases based on guesswork; understand the current phase and documented action.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">8</div><div><h4>Handle errors</h4><p>Capture the phase, error text, log name and timestamp. Fix the prerequisite or dependency, then continue according to the supported SUM procedure.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">9</div><div><h4>Post-maintenance validation</h4><p>Check SAP processes, dumps, system log, transports, jobs, RFCs, interfaces, certificates and key business transactions.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">10</div><div><h4>Close with evidence</h4><p>Record versions before/after, change number, validation results, open issues and rollback/contingency status.</p></div></div></div>\n    <h3>Key T-codes / tools</h3><div class=\"codechips\"><code>SNOTE</code><code>SPAM</code><code>SAINT</code><code>ST22</code><code>SM21</code><code>STMS</code></div>\n    <h3>Topics covered from your syllabus</h3><ul class=\"source-list\"><li>Software Maintenance</li><li>Implementing SAP Notes and SAP Support Packages</li><li>Preparing a software maintenance process</li><li>Working with the Support Package Manager (SPAM)</li><li>Understanding the Software Update Manager (SUM)</li><li>Exercise 15: SAP Note Assistant</li><li>Exercise 16: SAP Support Packages</li><li>Exercise 26: Update AS ABAP using SUM (Single System)</li></ul>\n    <h3>Interview answer</h3><div class=\"callout\">I describe maintenance as assess → plan → prepare → execute → monitor → validate → document. I emphasise that SUM errors should be handled from the exact phase/log rather than by repeated restarts.</div>\n    <h3>Production checklist</h3><ul><li>Capture exact timestamp and affected scope.</li><li>Check evidence before making a change.</li><li>Use approved change/incident procedures.</li><li>Validate the original business symptom after the fix.</li><li>Document RCA and preventive action for recurring issues.</li></ul>", "output-jobs": "<div class=\"detail-hero\"><img src=\"diagrams/spool-jobs.svg\" alt=\"Output Management, Spool &amp; Background Jobs diagram\"></div>\n    <h2>Output Management, Spool &amp; Background Jobs</h2>\n    <p class=\"lead\">Printer configuration, spool lifecycle and safe background-job administration.</p>\n    <h3>Step-by-step procedure</h3><div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">1</div><div><h4>Understand spool flow</h4><p>Separate SAP spool generation from the physical printing path. A successful spool request does not necessarily mean a paper/PDF output was delivered.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">2</div><div><h4>Check SP01</h4><p>Review request status, owner, creation time, output device and error information.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">3</div><div><h4>Check SPAD</h4><p>Validate output device configuration, access method, device type and print-server/host details.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">4</div><div><h4>Check host/network path</h4><p>For remote printing, verify the external print server or host path and network reachability.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">5</div><div><h4>Create a printer safely</h4><p>Collect device type, access method, host/printer details and business owner. Test with a controlled output.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">6</div><div><h4>Configure logical spool servers</h4><p>Use logical spool server concepts to distribute print workload and provide resilience where designed.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">7</div><div><h4>Manage spool growth</h4><p>Monitor old requests and retention policy. Delete only according to business and compliance requirements.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">8</div><div><h4>Analyse cancelled jobs in SM37</h4><p>Check job log, step, variant, spool and cancellation timestamp.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">9</div><div><h4>Correlate job failures</h4><p>Use ST22, SM21, SM12 and SM50/SM66 around the exact failure time.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">10</div><div><h4>Rerun safely</h4><p>Before rerunning a failed job, determine whether it partially processed data. Confirm duplicate-processing risk with the application owner.</p></div></div></div>\n    <h3>Key T-codes / tools</h3><div class=\"codechips\"><code>SP01</code><code>SPAD</code><code>SM37</code><code>SM50</code><code>SM12</code><code>ST22</code></div>\n    <h3>Topics covered from your syllabus</h3><ul class=\"source-list\"><li>Output Management</li><li>Printer Configuration</li><li>Background Job Processing</li><li>Scheduling Background jobs</li><li>Exercise 29: Check the Print Environment</li><li>Exercise 30: Create an Output Device using Host Spool Access Method Local</li><li>Exercise 31: Create an Output Device using Host Spool Access Method Remote</li><li>Exercise 32: Create an Output Device for Front-End Printing with SAP GUI for Windows</li><li>Exercise 33: Activate Unicode Printing Enhancement for your Output Device</li><li>Exercise 34: Set Up Logical Spool Servers</li><li>Exercise 35: Delete Spool Requests</li></ul>\n    <h3>Interview answer</h3><div class=\"callout\">For a cancelled job, I first identify the exact failing step and business impact, then correlate SM37 with ST22/SM21/locks and only rerun after confirming that duplicate processing will not occur.</div>\n    <h3>Production checklist</h3><ul><li>Capture exact timestamp and affected scope.</li><li>Check evidence before making a change.</li><li>Use approved change/incident procedures.</li><li>Validate the original business symptom after the fix.</li><li>Document RCA and preventive action for recurring issues.</li></ul>", "monitoring": "<div class=\"detail-hero\"><img src=\"diagrams/monitoring-troubleshooting.svg\" alt=\"System Monitoring, Troubleshooting &amp; Solution Manager diagram\"></div>\n    <h2>System Monitoring, Troubleshooting &amp; Solution Manager</h2>\n    <p class=\"lead\">A practical evidence-driven monitoring and troubleshooting methodology.</p>\n    <h3>Step-by-step procedure</h3><div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">1</div><div><h4>Define impact and scope</h4><p>Ask: who is affected, what transaction/process, when it started and whether it is intermittent or constant.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">2</div><div><h4>Establish a timestamp</h4><p>Use one incident timestamp and timezone. This is the key that lets you correlate SAP, HANA, OS and network evidence.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">3</div><div><h4>Check system log</h4><p>SM21 can reveal system-level events. Filter by server and time window.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">4</div><div><h4>Check dumps</h4><p>ST22 identifies ABAP runtime errors. Capture error name, program/include, line, user and timestamp.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">5</div><div><h4>Check work processes</h4><p>SM50 gives instance-level view; SM66 gives global view. Look for long-running processes, unusual states and wait reasons.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">6</div><div><h4>Check workload</h4><p>ST03N provides workload analysis; STAD gives detailed statistical records. Compare with a normal baseline.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">7</div><div><h4>Check locks and updates</h4><p>SM12 helps identify logical locks; SM13 helps inspect failed update requests.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">8</div><div><h4>Correlate database and OS</h4><p>Only after application evidence points there should you deep-dive into database/OS metrics. Check CPU, memory, filesystem and database time.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">9</div><div><h4>Validate the fix</h4><p>Retest the original business transaction and check monitoring for recurrence.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">10</div><div><h4>Write the RCA</h4><p>Document symptom, impact, timeline, evidence, immediate cause, root cause, corrective action and preventive action.</p></div></div></div>\n    <h3>Key T-codes / tools</h3><div class=\"codechips\"><code>SM21</code><code>ST22</code><code>SM50</code><code>SM66</code><code>ST03N</code><code>STAD</code><code>SM12</code><code>SM13</code><code>ST02</code><code>ST06</code><code>ST11</code></div>\n    <h3>Topics covered from your syllabus</h3><ul class=\"source-list\"><li>System Monitoring and Troubleshooting</li><li>Monitoring SAP Systems AS ABAP</li><li>Monitoring with SAP Solution Manager</li></ul>\n    <h3>Interview answer</h3><div class=\"callout\">My troubleshooting sequence is scope → timestamp → SAP logs → work processes → workload → locks/updates → DB/OS → fix → validation → RCA. This keeps the investigation evidence-driven.</div>\n    <h3>Production checklist</h3><ul><li>Capture exact timestamp and affected scope.</li><li>Check evidence before making a change.</li><li>Use approved change/incident procedures.</li><li>Validate the original business symptom after the fix.</li><li>Document RCA and preventive action for recurring issues.</li></ul>", "http": "<div class=\"detail-hero\"><img src=\"diagrams/http-webdispatcher.svg\" alt=\"HTTP Communication: ICM, ICF, Gateway &amp; Web Dispatcher diagram\"></div>\n    <h2>HTTP Communication: ICM, ICF, Gateway &amp; Web Dispatcher</h2>\n    <p class=\"lead\">HTTP/HTTPS components, SAP Gateway and Web Dispatcher configuration concepts.</p>\n    <h3>Step-by-step procedure</h3><div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">1</div><div><h4>Understand the path</h4><p>A typical request may travel browser → Web Dispatcher/load balancer → ICM → ICF service → ABAP application.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">2</div><div><h4>Check ICM</h4><p>Use SMICM to inspect status, services, connections and traces. Verify HTTP/HTTPS ports and recent changes.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">3</div><div><h4>Check ICF</h4><p>Use SICF to understand service activation and configuration. An inactive service can cause an application endpoint failure.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">4</div><div><h4>Understand Gateway</h4><p>SAP Gateway handles specific external communication scenarios and is monitored with SMGW. Do not confuse SAP Gateway with the HTTP Web Gateway concept.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">5</div><div><h4>Plan Web Dispatcher</h4><p>Define backend targets, routing rules, ports, TLS certificates and health monitoring.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">6</div><div><h4>Enable HTTPS</h4><p>Prepare the certificate/PSE chain, bind the appropriate service port and validate TLS from the actual client path.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">7</div><div><h4>Test end to end</h4><p>Test from browser/application consumer through the complete path rather than only testing the backend locally.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">8</div><div><h4>Troubleshoot with timestamps</h4><p>Compare browser/consumer error, Web Dispatcher trace, ICM trace and application logs.</p></div></div></div>\n    <h3>Key T-codes / tools</h3><div class=\"codechips\"><code>SMICM</code><code>SICF</code><code>SMGW</code><code>STRUST</code><code>ST11</code></div>\n    <h3>Topics covered from your syllabus</h3><ul class=\"source-list\"><li>Technology Components for HTTP-based Communication</li><li>Internet Communication Manager (ICM)</li><li>Internet Communication Framework (ICF)</li><li>Web Services and SAP Gateway in AS ABAP</li><li>SAP Web Dispatcher</li><li>Installing and Configuring SAP Web Dispatcher</li></ul>\n    <h3>Interview answer</h3><div class=\"callout\">For an HTTPS issue I trace the request hop by hop: client → Web Dispatcher → ICM → ICF/application. That prevents me from changing backend settings when the actual failure is at TLS or routing.</div>\n    <h3>Production checklist</h3><ul><li>Capture exact timestamp and affected scope.</li><li>Check evidence before making a change.</li><li>Use approved change/incident procedures.</li><li>Validate the original business symptom after the fix.</li><li>Document RCA and preventive action for recurring issues.</li></ul>", "smtp-ers": "<div class=\"detail-hero\"><img src=\"diagrams/smtp-ers.svg\" alt=\"SMTP, SAPconnect &amp; Enqueue Replication Server diagram\"></div>\n    <h2>SMTP, SAPconnect &amp; Enqueue Replication Server</h2>\n    <p class=\"lead\">Email flow and high-availability concepts around enqueue replication.</p>\n    <h3>Step-by-step procedure</h3><div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">1</div><div><h4>Map the email path</h4><p>SAP application → SAPconnect/SMTP configuration → SMTP relay/server → recipient. Identify where the failure occurs.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">2</div><div><h4>Check SAPconnect configuration</h4><p>Review SMTP node/configuration and scheduled send processes according to the installed release.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">3</div><div><h4>Test an email</h4><p>Send a controlled message and capture timestamp, sender, recipient and status.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">4</div><div><h4>Check logs and relay</h4><p>Correlate SAP-side status with SMTP relay/network evidence.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">5</div><div><h4>Understand enqueue</h4><p>The enqueue service manages SAP logical locks. It is a critical central service in the architecture.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">6</div><div><h4>Understand ERS</h4><p>ERS provides replicated enqueue information for high-availability designs. Exact architecture depends on SAP release and HA solution.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">7</div><div><h4>Validate failover design</h4><p>Test according to the HA runbook, not by ad-hoc stopping production services.</p></div></div></div>\n    <h3>Key T-codes / tools</h3><div class=\"codechips\"><code>SMICM</code><code>SM21</code><code>SM12</code></div>\n    <h3>Topics covered from your syllabus</h3><ul class=\"source-list\"><li>SMTP Communication and SAPconnect</li><li>Enqueue Replication Server (ERS)</li><li>Exercise 48: Send and Receive E-Mails Using SMTP with AS ABAP</li><li>Exercise: Using the Enqueue Replication Server (ERS)</li></ul>\n    <h3>Interview answer</h3><div class=\"callout\">I separate email generation from delivery. For ERS, I explain the business purpose first—protecting enqueue state during central-service failure—then discuss the actual HA architecture used by the customer.</div>\n    <h3>Production checklist</h3><ul><li>Capture exact timestamp and affected scope.</li><li>Check evidence before making a change.</li><li>Use approved change/incident procedures.</li><li>Validate the original business symptom after the fix.</li><li>Document RCA and preventive action for recurring issues.</li></ul>", "java": "<div class=\"detail-hero\"><img src=\"diagrams/java-architecture.svg\" alt=\"SAP NetWeaver AS Java Fundamentals &amp; Administration diagram\"></div>\n    <h2>SAP NetWeaver AS Java Fundamentals &amp; Administration</h2>\n    <p class=\"lead\">Java architecture, startup/stop, Config Tool, NWA, UME and Java monitoring.</p>\n    <h3>Step-by-step procedure</h3><div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">1</div><div><h4>Understand Java architecture</h4><p>Study the Java cluster model, central services and server processes as a separate runtime architecture from AS ABAP.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">2</div><div><h4>Identify administration tools</h4><p>Know the role of SAP NetWeaver Administrator and Config Tool, and which tool is appropriate for the configuration task.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">3</div><div><h4>Start/stop Java</h4><p>Use the supported startup/control framework and confirm the expected Java server processes reach a healthy state.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">4</div><div><h4>Read Java logs</h4><p>When startup fails, capture exact timestamps and inspect relevant default/server traces and log viewer information.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">5</div><div><h4>Configure basic properties</h4><p>Make controlled configuration changes through supported administration tools and document the previous value.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">6</div><div><h4>UME administration</h4><p>Understand users, groups and authorization concepts in the Java environment.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">7</div><div><h4>Destinations/JCo</h4><p>For Java Connector scenarios, verify destination properties, target reachability, credentials and connection tests.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">8</div><div><h4>Monitor availability</h4><p>Use monitoring/log tools and central monitoring integration where configured.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">9</div><div><h4>Maintain Java software</h4><p>Understand Java support packages/stacks/patches and the role of SUM/JSPM depending on release.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">10</div><div><h4>Backup and recovery</h4><p>Know what configuration/application data must be protected and follow the customer backup design.</p></div></div></div>\n    <h3>Key T-codes / tools</h3><div class=\"codechips\"><code>NWA</code><code>Config Tool</code><code>SM21</code></div>\n    <h3>Topics covered from your syllabus</h3><ul class=\"source-list\"><li>Fundamentals of SAP NW AS Java</li><li>Describing the Fundamental Concepts of SAP NW AS Java</li><li>Describing the Architecture of the SAP NW Java</li><li>Outlining the Java Cluster Architecture</li><li>Describing the Internal Structure of SAP NetWeaver AS Java</li><li>Start and Stop Procedure of SAP NW AS Java</li><li>Basic Configuration (SAP NW AS Java)</li><li>User and Authorization Administration (SAP NW AS Java)</li></ul>\n    <h3>Interview answer</h3><div class=\"callout\">For Java incidents I first identify the cluster/server component, then use the correct administration tool and exact log timestamp. I avoid changing multiple properties at once because that makes the root cause harder to prove.</div>\n    <h3>Production checklist</h3><ul><li>Capture exact timestamp and affected scope.</li><li>Check evidence before making a change.</li><li>Use approved change/incident procedures.</li><li>Validate the original business symptom after the fix.</li><li>Document RCA and preventive action for recurring issues.</li></ul>", "alm-cts": "<div class=\"detail-hero\"><img src=\"diagrams/cts-tms.svg\" alt=\"Application Lifecycle Management &amp; CTS/TMS diagram\"></div>\n    <h2>Application Lifecycle Management &amp; CTS/TMS</h2>\n    <p class=\"lead\">Landscape, transport requests, export/import, QA approval and monitoring.</p>\n    <h3>Step-by-step procedure</h3><div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">1</div><div><h4>Understand the landscape</h4><p>Document DEV, QAS and PRD systems, clients, transport domain and routes. The exact landscape varies by customer.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">2</div><div><h4>Create/record the request</h4><p>Customizing changes and development changes are captured in transport requests according to their object type and process.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">3</div><div><h4>Release correctly</h4><p>Before release, check object list, dependencies, task status and required approvals.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">4</div><div><h4>Export</h4><p>Release/export generates the files needed for downstream import according to the transport architecture.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">5</div><div><h4>Import into QAS</h4><p>Use STMS to select the correct request and review import options and history.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">6</div><div><h4>Validate in QAS</h4><p>Application testing and QA approval should happen before production movement.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">7</div><div><h4>Import into PRD</h4><p>Follow change-control approval, import sequence and business window.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">8</div><div><h4>Analyse RC 4/8/12</h4><p>RC 4 usually indicates warnings; RC 8 or higher requires investigation. Always inspect the detailed log.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">9</div><div><h4>Handle dependencies</h4><p>If a request fails because a prerequisite is missing, do not simply repeat the same import. Identify the dependency and correct the sequence.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">10</div><div><h4>Monitor and document</h4><p>Keep import history, approvals, validation evidence and rollback/contingency notes.</p></div></div></div>\n    <h3>Key T-codes / tools</h3><div class=\"codechips\"><code>STMS</code><code>SE10</code><code>SE09</code><code>STMS_IMPORT</code></div>\n    <h3>Topics covered from your syllabus</h3><ul class=\"source-list\"><li>Application lifecycle Management (ALM)</li><li>Introduction to Software Logistics Toolsets</li><li>SAP System landscape and client concept</li><li>Change &amp; Transport System (CTS) - Export Transport Requests</li><li>Creating, exporting, and importing transport requests</li><li>Customizing and Customizing projects</li><li>Transport Requests for development</li><li>Change &amp; Transport System (CTS) - Importing Transport Requests</li><li>Imports Using TMS</li><li>QA Approval Procedure and Transport Proposals</li><li>Import Process</li><li>Monitoring Tools</li></ul>\n    <h3>Interview answer</h3><div class=\"callout\">I describe transports as controlled software logistics, not just clicking Import. I validate the request, dependencies, sequence, approvals, detailed logs and post-import business testing.</div>\n    <h3>Production checklist</h3><ul><li>Capture exact timestamp and affected scope.</li><li>Check evidence before making a change.</li><li>Use approved change/incident procedures.</li><li>Validate the original business symptom after the fix.</li><li>Document RCA and preventive action for recurring issues.</li></ul>", "client-cts": "<div class=\"detail-hero\"><img src=\"diagrams/client-cts.svg\" alt=\"Client Administration, Enhanced CTS &amp; Non-ABAP Transport diagram\"></div>\n    <h2>Client Administration, Enhanced CTS &amp; Non-ABAP Transport</h2>\n    <p class=\"lead\">Client copy/compare concepts and enhanced transport scenarios.</p>\n    <h3>Step-by-step procedure</h3><div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">1</div><div><h4>Clarify the requirement</h4><p>A client copy is not the same as a complete system refresh. Determine whether data/configuration within a client or the entire system is being replaced.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">2</div><div><h4>Plan client copy</h4><p>Check source/target clients, data selection, downtime/locking implications, storage and post-copy tasks.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">3</div><div><h4>Post-copy actions</h4><p>Validate logical system, RFCs, jobs, printers, users, interfaces and client-dependent settings.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">4</div><div><h4>Compare clients</h4><p>Use the supported compare/maintenance tools to identify differences before changing anything.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">5</div><div><h4>Understand enhanced CTS</h4><p>Enhanced CTS extends transport concepts for additional object types/scenarios. Exact supported objects depend on release and configuration.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">6</div><div><h4>Non-ABAP transport</h4><p>Understand how non-ABAP objects can be transported through CTS-based processes when configured.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">7</div><div><h4>Validate end-to-end</h4><p>Test the object in the target system and document the transport result.</p></div></div></div>\n    <h3>Key T-codes / tools</h3><div class=\"codechips\"><code>SCC4</code><code>SCCL</code><code>SCC9</code><code>STMS</code></div>\n    <h3>Topics covered from your syllabus</h3><ul class=\"source-list\"><li>Client Administration</li><li>Client Copy and Client Transport Tools</li><li>Client Compare and Client Maintenance Tools</li><li>Enhanced CTS - The Basic Idea</li><li>Configuration of the Change and Transport System for the enhanced CTS</li><li>Transport of Non-ABAP Objects</li></ul>\n    <h3>Interview answer</h3><div class=\"callout\">My first question is always scope: are we refreshing a complete system or copying a client? That determines the tooling, impact and post-copy checklist.</div>\n    <h3>Production checklist</h3><ul><li>Capture exact timestamp and affected scope.</li><li>Check evidence before making a change.</li><li>Use approved change/incident procedures.</li><li>Validate the original business symptom after the fix.</li><li>Document RCA and preventive action for recurring issues.</li></ul>", "fiori": "<div class=\"detail-hero\"><img src=\"diagrams/fiori-architecture.svg\" alt=\"SAP Fiori: Infrastructure, Deployment, Security &amp; Operations diagram\"></div>\n    <h2>SAP Fiori: Infrastructure, Deployment, Security &amp; Operations</h2>\n    <p class=\"lead\">Fiori app types, launchpad, HTTPS, SSO, deployment options, search and operations.</p>\n    <h3>Step-by-step procedure</h3><div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">1</div><div><h4>Understand Fiori architecture</h4><p>Think browser → Fiori launchpad/UI layer → HTTP/ICF → ABAP/OData services → application/database.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">2</div><div><h4>Identify app type</h4><p>Transactional, analytical, fact sheet/reference and other app patterns have different backend/service dependencies.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">3</div><div><h4>Plan deployment</h4><p>Determine embedded vs other deployment architecture, system landscape, sizing, connectivity and transport needs.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">4</div><div><h4>Configure HTTPS</h4><p>Validate TLS certificate, ICM/Web Dispatcher and trust chain.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">5</div><div><h4>Configure launchpad</h4><p>Confirm launchpad services, catalogs/spaces/roles as applicable to the release and user authorizations.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">6</div><div><h4>Activate required services</h4><p>Identify the relevant ICF/OData services and activate/configure them through the supported process.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">7</div><div><h4>Plan SSO</h4><p>Determine the chosen identity/trust model and validate browser-to-SAP authentication.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">8</div><div><h4>Configure search/analytics</h4><p>Enterprise Search and embedded analytics have their own prerequisites and activation activities.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">9</div><div><h4>Transport Fiori content</h4><p>Include relevant configuration/development objects in the transport strategy.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">10</div><div><h4>Troubleshoot</h4><p>Use browser network errors, ICM, ICF, authorization checks and backend application logs to isolate the failing layer.</p></div></div></div>\n    <h3>Key T-codes / tools</h3><div class=\"codechips\"><code>SICF</code><code>SMICM</code><code>STRUST</code><code>SU01</code><code>ST22</code><code>STMS</code></div>\n    <h3>Topics covered from your syllabus</h3><ul class=\"source-list\"><li>SAP Fiori Introduction</li><li>SAP Fiori App Types</li><li>Implementation Planning</li><li>Fiori Deployment Options</li><li>SAP Fiori System Landscape</li><li>Enterprise Search Activation</li><li>Embedded Analytics Activation</li><li>HTTPS Communication Channel</li><li>Single Sign-On</li><li>SAP Fiori Infrastructure</li><li>Rapid Activation</li><li>SAP Fiori Launchpad</li><li>In-App Help</li><li>Notifications</li><li>SAP Fiori App Implementation</li><li>Transactional Apps</li><li>Reference Apps</li><li>Analytical Apps</li><li>Fact Sheet Apps and SAP Fiori Search</li><li>SAP Fiori Operations</li><li>Transport Concepts</li><li>Troubleshooting</li><li>Monitoring</li></ul>\n    <h3>Interview answer</h3><div class=\"callout\">I troubleshoot Fiori from the browser backwards: URL/TLS → Web Dispatcher/ICM → ICF/OData → authorization → backend application. This avoids changing the wrong layer.</div>\n    <h3>Production checklist</h3><ul><li>Capture exact timestamp and affected scope.</li><li>Check evidence before making a change.</li><li>Use approved change/incident procedures.</li><li>Validate the original business symptom after the fix.</li><li>Document RCA and preventive action for recurring issues.</li></ul>", "hana": "<div class=\"detail-hero\"><img src=\"diagrams/hana-mdc.svg\" alt=\"SAP HANA Overview, Architecture, MDC &amp; Administration diagram\"></div>\n    <h2>SAP HANA Overview, Architecture, MDC &amp; Administration</h2>\n    <p class=\"lead\">HANA fundamentals, requirements, MDC, Studio/Cockpit, administration and lifecycle.</p>\n    <h3>Step-by-step procedure</h3><div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">1</div><div><h4>Understand HANA architecture</h4><p>Learn the in-memory columnar database concept, persistence, services and how SAP application workloads use HANA.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">2</div><div><h4>Understand MDC</h4><p>A HANA system can contain a SystemDB and one or more tenant databases. SystemDB handles system-level administration; tenant DBs host application data.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">3</div><div><h4>Prepare installation</h4><p>Validate certified OS, memory/storage sizing, hostname/network, users, filesystems and required media for the target release.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">4</div><div><h4>Install using supported procedure</h4><p>Follow the release-specific SAP HANA installation method and record system identifiers, instance number and database names.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">5</div><div><h4>Perform post-install checks</h4><p>Confirm services, ports, users, filesystem layout, time synchronization, backup configuration and monitoring.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">6</div><div><h4>Use HANA Cockpit/Studio where supported</h4><p>Connect to the appropriate database and use the administration views relevant to health, performance and configuration.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">7</div><div><h4>Monitor memory</h4><p>Identify memory consumers and workload patterns. Avoid interpreting a high memory number without understanding HANA's allocation/usage model.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">8</div><div><h4>Monitor disk</h4><p>Check data/log filesystem usage and growth. Never remove database-managed files manually.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">9</div><div><h4>Lifecycle management</h4><p>Plan revisions/updates with compatibility, backup, downtime and application validation.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">10</div><div><h4>Validate SAP on HANA</h4><p>Correlate HANA health with SAP transactions, dumps, work processes and response time.</p></div></div></div>\n    <h3>Key T-codes / tools</h3><div class=\"codechips\"><code>DBACOCKPIT</code><code>DB02</code><code>ST04</code><code>ST03N</code></div>\n    <h3>Topics covered from your syllabus</h3><ul class=\"source-list\"><li>SAP HANA Overview</li><li>Describing SAP HANA</li><li>Technical Requirements of SAP HANA</li><li>Data Management with SAP HANA</li><li>Analytical Processing with SAP HANA</li><li>Running SAP Enterprise Suites on SAP HANA</li><li>Migration to SAP HANA</li><li>SAP HANA Introduction</li><li>Installation Preparations</li><li>SAP HANA Installation</li><li>Multitenant Database Container Architecture</li><li>Working with SAP HANA Studio (Administrative Perspective)</li><li>SAP HANA Cockpit 2.0 Overview, Installation and Configuration</li><li>SAP HANA Administration tools</li><li>Post installation steps for SAP HANA</li><li>Working on HANA Lifecycle Management</li></ul>\n    <h3>Interview answer</h3><div class=\"callout\">For HANA I focus on health, memory, disk, backups, SQL/workload, replication and recovery. I always separate SystemDB administration from tenant application workload.</div>\n    <h3>Production checklist</h3><ul><li>Capture exact timestamp and affected scope.</li><li>Check evidence before making a change.</li><li>Use approved change/incident procedures.</li><li>Validate the original business symptom after the fix.</li><li>Document RCA and preventive action for recurring issues.</li></ul>", "hana-backup-security": "<div class=\"detail-hero\"><img src=\"diagrams/hana-backup-security.svg\" alt=\"HANA Backup, Recovery, Security, Users, Roles &amp; Auditing diagram\"></div>\n    <h2>HANA Backup, Recovery, Security, Users, Roles &amp; Auditing</h2>\n    <p class=\"lead\">Backup/recovery strategy and HANA security administration.</p>\n    <h3>Step-by-step procedure</h3><div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">1</div><div><h4>Define RPO/RTO</h4><p>Before choosing a backup approach, understand how much data loss is acceptable and how quickly the service must return.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">2</div><div><h4>Check backup configuration</h4><p>Verify data and log backup destinations, schedule, retention and monitoring.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">3</div><div><h4>Monitor backup success</h4><p>A configured backup is not enough; confirm recent successful backups and test that the backup chain is usable.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">4</div><div><h4>Plan recovery</h4><p>Document the recovery sequence, prerequisites, target point-in-time and post-recovery SAP validation.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">5</div><div><h4>Test restore</h4><p>Periodic restore testing is what proves recoverability. A backup that has never been restored is an assumption.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">6</div><div><h4>Create least-privilege users</h4><p>Use named administrative users and appropriate technical users. Avoid broad privileges unless justified.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">7</div><div><h4>Manage roles</h4><p>Separate role design from user assignment. Review privileged access periodically.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">8</div><div><h4>Configure auditing</h4><p>Define which security-relevant activities need audit coverage and retain logs according to policy.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">9</div><div><h4>Protect credentials</h4><p>Use secure credential storage and approved password/secret handling.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">10</div><div><h4>Validate security after changes</h4><p>Test required application connectivity and confirm no unintended privilege or certificate changes occurred.</p></div></div></div>\n    <h3>Key T-codes / tools</h3><div class=\"codechips\"><code>DBACOCKPIT</code><code>STRUST</code><code>SU01</code></div>\n    <h3>Topics covered from your syllabus</h3><ul class=\"source-list\"><li>Database Administration tasks using SAP HANA Studio &amp; SAP HANA cockpit 2.0</li><li>Backup and Recovery using SAP HANA cockpit 2.0</li><li>SAP HANA Security</li><li>Users and Role Management</li><li>HANA Auditing</li><li>Working on HANA Security using HANA Cockpit 2.0</li></ul>\n    <h3>Interview answer</h3><div class=\"callout\">I answer backup questions in terms of RPO/RTO, backup success, restore testing and business validation—not simply &#x27;we take daily backups&#x27;.</div>\n    <h3>Production checklist</h3><ul><li>Capture exact timestamp and affected scope.</li><li>Check evidence before making a change.</li><li>Use approved change/incident procedures.</li><li>Validate the original business symptom after the fix.</li><li>Document RCA and preventive action for recurring issues.</li></ul>", "hana-migration": "<div class=\"detail-hero\"><img src=\"diagrams/hana-migration.svg\" alt=\"Migration to SAP HANA diagram\"></div>\n    <h2>Migration to SAP HANA</h2>\n    <p class=\"lead\">A BASIS-focused migration lifecycle: assessment, preparation, execution and validation.</p>\n    <h3>Step-by-step procedure</h3><div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">1</div><div><h4>Assess the source</h4><p>Capture SAP release, database, custom code, interfaces, add-ons, data volume, workload and business criticality.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">2</div><div><h4>Confirm target compatibility</h4><p>Validate supported SAP/HANA versions, operating system, sizing and required components.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">3</div><div><h4>Choose the migration approach</h4><p>The method depends on the starting platform, target architecture, downtime tolerance and SAP-supported options. DMO of SUM is one common approach for eligible scenarios.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">4</div><div><h4>Prepare infrastructure</h4><p>Provision HANA resources, filesystems, network, DNS, OS prerequisites, users and monitoring.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">5</div><div><h4>Prepare SUM/maintenance stack</h4><p>Obtain supported software, stack information and required media; resolve prerequisites before the execution window.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">6</div><div><h4>Validate backups</h4><p>Take and verify backups and define contingency/rollback decisions.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">7</div><div><h4>Execute migration</h4><p>Monitor SUM/DMO phases, database migration activities, logs and resource usage.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">8</div><div><h4>Perform technical validation</h4><p>Check HANA services, SAP processes, dumps, logs, jobs, RFCs, interfaces, printers and users.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">9</div><div><h4>Perform performance validation</h4><p>Compare critical transaction/workload performance against baseline and investigate regressions.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">10</div><div><h4>Hypercare and RCA</h4><p>Increase monitoring, capture issues, tune where justified and document lessons learned.</p></div></div></div>\n    <h3>Key T-codes / tools</h3><div class=\"codechips\"><code>ST03N</code><code>STAD</code><code>ST22</code><code>SM21</code><code>SM37</code><code>SM59</code><code>STMS</code></div>\n    <h3>Topics covered from your syllabus</h3><ul class=\"source-list\"><li>Migration to SAP HANA</li><li>Running SAP Enterprise Suites on SAP HANA</li><li>SAP HANA Installation</li></ul>\n    <h3>Interview answer</h3><div class=\"callout\">I present migration as a lifecycle, not a single technical command: assess → design → prepare → precheck → execute → validate → hypercare.</div>\n    <h3>Production checklist</h3><ul><li>Capture exact timestamp and affected scope.</li><li>Check evidence before making a change.</li><li>Use approved change/incident procedures.</li><li>Validate the original business symptom after the fix.</li><li>Document RCA and preventive action for recurring issues.</li></ul>", "global-tools": "<div class=\"detail-hero\"><img src=\"diagrams/globalization.svg\" alt=\"CUA, eCATT &amp; Globalization diagram\"></div>\n    <h2>CUA, eCATT &amp; Globalization</h2>\n    <p class=\"lead\">Central user administration, automated testing concepts and multi-language considerations.</p>\n    <h3>Step-by-step procedure</h3><div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">1</div><div><h4>Understand CUA</h4><p>Identify the central system and child systems, distribution model and synchronization responsibilities.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">2</div><div><h4>Plan user changes</h4><p>Decide which user attributes are centrally governed and which are local. Test synchronization in non-production.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">3</div><div><h4>Monitor distribution</h4><p>When a user change does not appear in a child system, check distribution status, RFC connectivity and target-side logs.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">4</div><div><h4>Understand eCATT</h4><p>eCATT can support automated testing/configuration scenarios. Use controlled test data and isolate test effects.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">5</div><div><h4>Plan globalization</h4><p>Multiple languages affect logon language, texts, date/number formats and application behavior.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">6</div><div><h4>Validate translations</h4><p>Check required language availability and test business transactions in supported languages.</p></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"stepno\">7</div><div><h4>Document operational ownership</h4><p>Centralised administration only works when responsibilities, connectivity and error handling are clearly defined.</p></div></div></div>\n    <h3>Key T-codes / tools</h3><div class=\"codechips\"><code>SU01</code><code>SUIM</code><code>SM59</code></div>\n    <h3>Topics covered from your syllabus</h3><ul class=\"source-list\"><li>Central User Administration (CUA)</li><li>Extended Computer Aided Test Tool (eCATT)</li><li>Aspects of Globalization</li><li>Multiple languages in one SAP system</li></ul>\n    <h3>Interview answer</h3><div class=\"callout\">I explain CUA as central governance plus distributed execution. When synchronization fails, I check the distribution path and RFC evidence instead of manually changing every child system.</div>\n    <h3>Production checklist</h3><ul><li>Capture exact timestamp and affected scope.</li><li>Check evidence before making a change.</li><li>Use approved change/incident procedures.</li><li>Validate the original business symptom after the fix.</li><li>Document RCA and preventive action for recurring issues.</li></ul>"};

